* bc547.lib - the tach output transistor Q4
* ------------------------------------------------------------------
* Q4 is the BC547 (TO-92) in cabinet R1 - D3.  It is an open-collector
* switch: the NUC's own pull-up makes the high side of the tach line,
* Q4 only ever sinks it.  Nothing here needs a vendor model - what has
* to be checked is that 2.6 mA of base drive saturates the part hard
* against the few milliamps a tach pull-up moves, and that it turns on
* and off fast enough to follow the carrier.
*
* Level-1 (Gummel-Poon) parameters, read off the BC547B datasheet:
*   hFE 200-450 (B bin)          -> bf = 200 (the low end: pessimistic drive)
*   Vce(sat) 0.2 V at Ic 2 mA    -> falls out of the model at Ib = 2.6 mA
*   Ic max 100 mA                -> ikf = 0.1 A
*   Vceo 45 V                    -> irrelevant here, the line sits at 5 V
*   Cjc 9 pF, Cje 12 pF, fT 100 MHz
*
* Good for: saturation depth, base current, switching speed.
* Not good for: quoting a precise hFE - real parts spread 110 to 800.
* (KiCad's model reader wants the whole .model on one line.)
.model bc547 npn (bf=200 is=2e-14 nf=1 ikf=0.1 vaf=100 rb=10 cje=12p tf=0.3n cjc=9p tr=5n isc=1e-14 nbr=1)


* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*  Sub-circuit wrapper - why this exists
*
*  The schematic symbol numbers its pins the way the package's legs are
*  numbered, because KiCad connects symbol pin N to footprint pad N.  KiCad
*  then writes the SPICE line in symbol pin order, so the wrapper's .subckt
*  signature is written in that same package order and re-orders the terminals
*  here for ngspice (which wants MOSFET as D G S [B] and BJT as C B E).
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.subckt npn_bc547 E B C
Q_int C B E bc547
*  TO-92 legs, seen from the flat face with the leads down: 1 emitter, 2 base,
*  3 collector.
.ends
